#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116480 :tutorial/content "When we transform a logarithmic function\n\n$$\ny = \\log_a (x)\n$$\n\nits domain might be modified as well.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\log_2(x-1)\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of $f(x)$?\n\n![](images/f1/f135e52b40df0923dea0bfd58a3255a096cab4ab23faa7c761ac5998fdfdd782.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the domain of\n\n$$\ny=\\log_a (x)\n$$\n\nis\n$x > 0$.\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)$, we take the above inequality and replace $x$ with the argument (or input) of the logarithm function contained in $f(x)$. In this case, the argument is $x-1$, so we get\n$x-1 > 0$.\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x - 1 & > 0 \\\\ x & > 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in(1, \\infty)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "345c9fab-a753-50d1-8833-12d8b2699fdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2817 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116527 :tutorial/content "When we transform a logarithmic function\n\n$$\ny = \\log_a (x)\n$$\n\nits range is not affected.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3\\log_2(x-1)+1\n$$\n\n![](images/4a/4a5bad1159efe184b0afc2731ebd6b5dd6149a0a1ba2d530cfdc769ab2acf250.png)\n\nThe range of $\\log_2 x$ is\n$\\log_2 x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nBecause the range of the logarithm function is the interval containing all real numbers, horizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect its range. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c2975532-f898-5fd3-a901-a0d8f037906f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4589 :tutorial/title "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}]}
